- "aka": other names for this {{ kind }} itself, if it has any, each {"name", "tag", "everyday"}. "tag" is "proper" for another formal name, or "common" for what people in the world actually call it day to day (a short form, a nickname). Set "everyday" true on the one common name people use most, false on the rest. "name" stays the most formal name. An empty list is fine.
- "names": after the facts, every proper name your facts use, other than this {{ kind }}'s own: people, places, peoples and species, named events, named groups and named objects, new or already in the world. Each is {"name", "kind", "tag", "in", "aka"}: "name" in full, as your facts write it; "kind" one of character, place, species, race, event, item, lore (a country, realm or city is a place; a faith, order, council, guild, culture, field of knowledge or magic, law, script or language is lore); "tag" what it is, as for the {{ kind }} (never "proper" or "common": those are only for other names); "in" the name of the entry it belongs in (this {{ kind }}, an entry listed above, or another of your names), or null; "aka" any other names your facts use for the same thing. Proper names only: never a description such as "the postmaster", "a war" or "his mother", nor a title on its own such as "the Tsar" or "the High Bishop", nor a people's adjective such as "Moscavian" on its own -- but "Yelena" in "his mother Yelena" is a name. An empty list is fine.
- Only named things become entries. A role, rank or office (a harbour master, the keeper of the seals), a topic (grain tariffs, winter trade) or a practice without a proper name of its own (the evening bell, night sessions) is never one of the "names": it stays a fact of the {{ kind }}.
{%- if nearby and not heritage_label %}
- Keep "facts" to claims mainly about the {{ kind }} itself: a claim mainly about another entry goes in "elsewhere".
- "elsewhere": after the names, what you would add about other entries, each {"entry", "text", "held_by"}: "entry" the entry's name as the list of entries near this one writes it, or a new name you also list in "names"; "text" one claim in a full sentence. Never the {{ kind }} itself. An empty list is fine.
- A claim that ties another entry to the {{ kind }} ("it is taught at X", "unlike X, it...", "X uses it") is the {{ kind }}'s own: it goes in "facts". "elsewhere" is only for what another entry's page should say even if the {{ kind }} didn't exist.
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind not in ("species", "race") %}
- "tag": one or two words for what the {{ kind }} is, such as river, ruler, custom, institution, regiment, capital or doctrine.{% if tags %} Use a tag from the tags in use when one fits; make a new one only when none does.{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if nearby %}
- "in": the entries listed above that the {{ kind }} belongs in, each {"entry", "role"}: "entry" the entry's name as the list writes it; "role" a few words for how it belongs there ("flows through", "sovereign", "practitioner", "branch of", "taught here"), or null. A thing can be in several entries: a road in each realm it crosses, a temple in its city and in the faith it serves. Only entries from the list; an empty list is fine.
- "file_into": the entries listed above that belong in this {{ kind }}: its parts, members, branches or what it holds, each {"entry", "role"}, "role" how that entry belongs in this {{ kind }}, or null. Never an entry you put in "in". Only entries from the list; an empty list is fine.
{%- endif %}
{%- if "belief" in categories %}
- "belief": every fact that says what the {{ kind }} teaches, holds or believes ("it teaches that...", "the faithful hold that...", "its doctrine says...") is a "belief", never "general". Plain truth of the world -- its rites as practised, its clergy, places, ranks and history -- goes in the other categories.
{%- endif %}
{%- if homes %}
- "exceptions": in an "in" item, only where this character departs from that entry, each {"text", "rejects"}: "text" one sentence on what they reject or do differently, and "rejects" the label of the item it departs from, such as "S3". The character already holds everything else the entry holds, so never restate it, in an exception or in the facts. Most entries have no exception or one.
- Write each exception only there, never again as one of the facts.
{%- endif %}

